dot-git(1) -- Interact with the local dotfile Git repository
============================================================

## SYNOPSIS

`dot git` [-h] <action> [<args>]

## DESCRIPTION

Interact with the local dotfile Git repository. As a general rule (unless specifically stated otherwise), this only interacts with managed dotfiles (i.e. those listed in managed.files), not the actual source code of **dot**. This is to make it very fast and easy to commit, push, and pull changes made to dotfiles.

Part of dot(1).

## OPTIONS

  * `-h`, `--h`:
    Show command help.

  * <action>:
    See [ACTIONS][].

  * [<args>]:
    Argument(s) passed to <action> which may or may not be optional, depending on the action.

## ACTIONS

`-h` always means "show command help".

  * `commit` [-h] [-m <message>]:
    Commit the changes made to managed dotfiles. Unless `-m` is passed, the commit message will be automatically generated. You will be asked to confirm the commit interactively.

    * `-m` <message>:
      Optionally specify the commit message. If not passed, a message will automatically generated.


  * `push`:
    Push the changes made to remote.


  * `pull`:
    Stash changes in local repo, pull changes from remote, then unstash changes.

    _NOTE:_ This might be a bit finicky, so use with caution. Also, it might exit non-zero if there's nothing to stash (and then unstash), but that's okay.


  * `undo`:
    Undo the last commit and unstage the previously committed files.

    _NOTE:_ While this has been tested, it might be a bit finicky, so use with caution.


  * `status`:
    Get the current Git status of the managed dotfiles.
    &nbsp;


  * `diff` [-h] [-a] [<file>...]:
    Get the current Git diff of the given files.

    * [<file>...]:
      Relative path to the file(s) to diff (optional). If not given, the diff of all managed dotfiles will be shown.

    * `-a`, `--all`:
      Include the diff of non-dotfiles (i.e. source code and documentation)

